<!--Inspect Documentation — Introduction
  FQN: org.blockml.bml.inspect.documentation.Introduction-->
<inspectdocumentation:Introduction xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:core="org.blockml.bml.core"
  xmlns:inspectcap="org.blockml.bml.inspect.capability"
  xmlns:inspectdocumentation="org.blockml.bml.inspect.documentation">

  <baseType>
    core:Documentation
  </baseType>
  <is>
    Purpose and scope of the Inspect package
  </is>
  <documentation>
    The @blockml/inspect package is an LLM read tool. Given a Block FQN it returns a compact JSON projection of the BOM: effective members grouped as properties, aggregations, associations, and capabilities, type refs, and optional recursive expansion of declared member types.
    
    Search answers where something is. Graph answers how it connects. Inspect answers: give me this part of the model already resolved so I can understand it.
    
    Member documentation is the first entry when present, truncated like Block documentation. Aggregation entries include authored composition instances. Authored documentation see references are compact FQN + relation and are not expanded; at most 8, with referencesTruncated as the remaining count. Long documentation is truncated with an ellipsis and documentationTruncated.
    
    The inspect view is ephemeral. It is not a canonical model format and is not written back to BML. This library (org.blockml.bml.inspect.*) is the SSOT. TypeScript in packages/inspect/src/ implements it.
  </documentation>
  <references>
    <see type="inspectdocumentation:LayerSeparation" relation="ReadNext" />
    <see type="inspectcap:InspectBlock" relation="SeeAlso" />
  </references>
</inspectdocumentation:Introduction>
